WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
This is the Roe River. And if you believe
the hype, it is the shortest river in the world.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.270
And through the miracle of modern technology,

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.090
the waterproof camera on this stick,

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.710
I can show you the full length of it.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:15.590
All the way from the source, a crystal-clear
spring in Giant Springs State Park, Montana,

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:17.570
flowing down 61 metres

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:22.390
until it joins the much, much, much larger
Missouri River flowing to the ocean.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:23.940
Yeah, the river's just there.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.400
And it really is crystal clear:
while I was setting up,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.980
someone came along with some bottles and
filled them up for drinking water.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.510
I was off in Vidcon in Los Angeles last week
as I record this,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.859
and I was having dinner with a few folks who
make educational videos

00:00:35.859 --> 00:00:39.289
when I mentioned that I was coming here to
talk about 'the shortest river'.

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:42.440
And if I'm honest, the conversation
didn't go well.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.610
They kept raising different objections,

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.620
and seemed to get angry just at the concept.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:52.010
So eventually I looked up the dictionary definition
of 'river' in the Oxford English Dictionary:

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.799
"A large natural stream of water
flowing in  a channel

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.840
"to the sea, a lake, or another, usually larger,
stream of the same kind."

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.880
First objection: this is man-made.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.000
True, the course of this was
refined by people, but...

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.420
...so was the Los Angeles River and that's
still a river

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.760
despite a lot of people thinking it's just
an overgrown storm drain.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.880
Although to be fair, the Roe River here didn't
even have a name until the 80s

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.560
when some students surveyed it and campaigned
for it to be named the shortest river.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.260
The Guinness Book of Records did
 eventually agree, at least for a while.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:28.560
Okay, said the folks I was talking to,
but how do you know it's the shortest?

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.860
That is a fair point.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.280
There have been long-documented arguments
about whether this river

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.360
is even the shortest
in the United States...

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:36.970
...and there are international claims too,

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:41.509
like this short channel between two lakes
near Jyväskylä in Finland.

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:44.219
And I was really skeptical before I got here,
but yeah,

00:01:44.219 --> 00:01:48.900
now I'm standing by it, I genuinely think
this is the shortest river in the world.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:54.259
It is a large, natural channel flowing in
one direction to a lake.

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:57.689
Fits all the dictionary definitions, unless
you want to argue that it's not large enough,

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:01.929
but frankly if it's large enough to drown in,
I think it should count.

00:02:01.929 --> 00:02:03.350
You could try adding more specifics.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.399
Forget arguing about whether this is natural:

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:08.700
how about saying that a river
starts at a source,

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.230
flows somewhere in a consistent direction,

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.160
and ends at a much bigger bit of water?

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.230
That seemed like a great definition to me,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.780
until someone pointed out that
that would include waterslides.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.370
Every time we tried to nail down a definition,
it slipped away.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:30.560
I'll be honest, I'm not really here to show you
The World's Shortest River, because I 
 can't.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.540
Because there will always be quibbles about
definitions.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:36.990
All the categories that we try to put the
natural world into,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:42.460
to fit everything into maps and databases,
all those categories have to be fuzzy.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:46.130
Mapmakers have known for years that, as with
so many things in the real world,

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:51.490
"is it a river" is not always a question that
can be answered with a binary 'yes' or 'no'.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:54.580
There is almost always
an exception to the  rule.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.500
The 'river' is whatever humans agree it is.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.151
The exact definition probably differs between
languages and cultures anyway.

00:03:01.151 --> 00:03:06.780
And in the end, the Guinness Book of Records
decided not to have a listing for shortest 
 river.

